  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia collaborated with the Solomon Islands Ministry of Health and Medical Services and Beyond Essential Systems to implement the National Supply Chain Reform project. This project aims to improve the skills of local personnel and enhance crucial operational processes within the health supply chain. Between January and June 2024, BES conducted mSupply training for 93 national staff across 9 provinces and Honiara City. The Second Level Medical Store in Isabel Province was upgraded from mSupply mobile to mSupply desktop, enabling pharmacy staff in Isabel to efficiently access reports and manage stock distribution to remote clinics.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia collaborated with the Solomon Islands Ministry of Health and Medical Services to renovate the Kukum Area Health Clinic in East Honiara, Solomon Islands. The Kukum Clinic, an Area Health Centre in the capital, provides outpatient care, child welfare, maternal health, and family planning, but has been closed since December 2023 due to the unsafe condition of the building. Australia&apos;s investment enabled building repairs and renovation and procurement of essential equipment. With Australia&#x2019;s support, the Kukum Area Health Clinic will reopen soon, providing essential health services to the large community in East Honiara and taking pressure off the National Referral Hospital.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia partnered with UNICEF and Solomon Islands Ministry of Health and Medical Services to improve access to sanitation services through the National Sustainable Sanitation Plan (NSSP).  Through the support provided to the NSSP, sanitation services were improved for 10,719 individuals from 64 communities in Malaita, the Central Islands and Western Province between 1 July 2024 and 30 June 2025. This highlights Australia&#x2019;s strong commitment as a trusted health development partner to the Solomon Islands.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia partnered with the Solomon Islands Ministry of Health and Medical Services (MHMS) to improve medical supply chains at the National Medical Store in Honiara and Second Level Medical Stores across the nine provinces. Beyond Essential Systems (BES) supported MHMS to deliver the National Supply Chain Reform project to target four key challenges - storage, payment arrears, port bottlenecks, and data quality. Australia&#x2019;s investment is contributing to a more responsive and effective health supply chain through, for example, capability development of local staff, improved efficiency of unloading stock containers, consolidation of two stock warehouses into one new warehouse, training of 54 national staff on Supply and procurement, supporting Provincial Pharmacy Officers to tour to every primary healthcare facility and complete assessments, software upgrades across 23 facilities and provision of 30 computing devices to the National Dental and Lab program.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia procured equipment valued at AUD73,046 to support establishment of a new 14 bed High Dependency Unit at the National Referral Hospital, Honiara as part of the Solomon Islands&#x2019; COVID-19 preparedness and response. The new equipment was underpinned by targeted training delivered through Australian-funded technical support. A nursing training manual was developed to support transition from a novice to practitioner and included competency-based assessment and adult learning principles. Intensive medical and nursing training was delivered over four weeks to 28 nurses, increasing the quality of clinical care in Solomon Islands.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia provided AUD21,717.60 to Australia Solomon Islands Resource Facility (ASIRF) to procure i) emergency High Dependency Unit rations (AUD1,016.60) and ii) repair the standby generator (AUD20,701) at the National Referral Hospital (NRH).</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Australia supported the MHMS to procure three prefabricated laboratories, with two completed and delivered in 2021/22 (Helena Goldie, Western Province; Taro, Choiseul Province) inclusive of furniture and fittings, equipment (GeneXpert machine, biochemistry analyzer, autoclave, microscopes, centrifuge, blood bank freezers etc) and reagents. Laboratory technicians from each location were trained to use the new equipment. In addition, Australia supported the MHMS to access pathology analysis testing services from Pathology Queensland and ongoing technical support from Doherty Institute. These initiatives are improving access to a broader range of diagnostic testing, reducing turnaround times and improving response capacity during emergencies.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">For the Measles and Rubella Supplementary Immunization Activity (SIA) implemented in 2022, Australia contributed SBD863,593/AUD156,015.57 to the Ministry of Health and Medical Services, Expanded Program of Immunization (EPI) to support micro-planning, social mobilization, and implementation in Malaita, Isabel and Honiara. 
&#x200d;
During the Measles and Rubella SIA, 5,783 children were immunised. No sex aggregated data is available. Australia&#x2019;s contribution to the Measles and Rubella SIA is 100%.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In late 2024, Australia responded to a request from the Solomon Islands Ministry of Health and Medical Services (MHMS) to support urgent procurement of essential medicines and medical supplies in response to existing and projected shortages. Australia&#x2019;s contribution of over AUD1.6 million enabled the MHMS to progress the procurement, which included diagnostic tests and treatments for a range of communicable disease, such as malaria, sexually transmitted infections and gastroenteritis. With Australia&#x2019;s assistance, the medication and supplies were delivered and distributed across the country, assisting Solomon Islands to manage high burden of communicable diseases.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Solomon Islands hosted the 17th Pacific Games in November 2023, with more than 10,000 athletes and spectators travelling to Solomon Islands from around the Pacific. Australia collaborated with the Ministry of Health and Medical Services, UNICEF, WHO and World Bank to implement a nationwide measles and rubella campaign in the lead up to the event. 71,447 children aged 6 months &#x2013; 4 years 11 months in all nine provinces and Honiara City Council were immunised, representing 94.4% coverage. The activity was successful in protecting the population and no measles outbreak occurred during or after the event.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">The World Scabies Program, in partnership with Australia and Solomon Islands Ministry of Health and Medical Services successfully completed its second round of national mass drug administration (MDA) for scabies control in December 2024. Solomon Islands is the first country in the world to complete two consecutive rounds of national MDA for scabies. With Australia&#x2019;s support (AUD2.9 million), nearly 70% of Solomon Islanders (over 487,000 people) received scabies medication during the MDA, with strong coverage across the nine provinces.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Health</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">WHO Solomon Islands Country Office, through Australia&#x2019;s WHO partnership, supported the Ministry of Health and Medical Services (MHMS) to improve maternal healthcare outcomes in response to four maternal deaths in Gizo, Western Province. WHO technical experts supported Emergency Management of Obstetric Complications training, attended by 28 nurses and midwives from Gizo Hospital, and helped develop the first draft of the Solomon Islands Postnatal Care Guidelines. WHO supported distribution of 1000 copies of the Solomon Islands Standard Treatment Manual for Obstetrics and Gynaecology, helping standardize and improve quality of care by enhancing competency and confidence of midwives and nurses.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
